ok ladies and gentlemen good morning and  welcome to this high-level public  conference on implementing the European  Green Deal and on the European climate  law which I know you're all waiting for  which is coming very soon my name is Tim  McPhee and the European Commission  spokesperson for climate action and  energy and I'm your MC for the day so  it'll be my pleasure to introduce to you  our great panel panels our keynote  speakers who are going to talk to you  about the climate law about the Green  Deal and of course who are here to  listen to you as well and this is an  interactive event we'll be taking  questions from the floor throughout all  the panels and of course you have the  coffee breaks the lunch breaks to get to  know each other as well and to work  together on the Green Deal this is not a  one-way conversation a two-way  conversation you're here to build new  relationships new partnerships this is  something which we're all going to be  working on together for the next 5 10 15  20 years and beyond we started a few  minutes late I think there was a bit of  a bottleneck downstairs and I know  there's been a few problems on the  public transport this morning which  means I don't have to talk and I can  straight away introduce you to our first  speaker this morning I'm sure all of you  know him already but I will introduce  him the executive vice president of the  European Commission for the European  Green Deal mr. Franz timmermans and  France please  good morning everyone and welcome to  what I hope is going to be an exciting  day I'm certainly looking forward also  to hearing the conclusions afterwards  this afternoon because we certainly  couldn't use all the help we can get and  if this is to be a a success it can only  happen if it is inclusive if you're all  part of it and if we can agree on the  track to follow  I think you've followed us closely over  the last couple of months we promised to  do a lot in the first 100 days of this  commission and part of that will be the  introduction of the European climate law  so that we can enshrine into law  objective to become climate neutral by  2050 you've seen the presentation of the  Green Deal communication you've probably  also followed the Green Deal investment  plan and adjust transition mechanism  which we introduced a couple of weeks  ago so we're not wasting any time  and by the way a big thank you to  everyone in the service of the  Commission who has contributed to this  we're putting people on the tremendous  pressure but we'll make sure that what  we deliver it continues to be of high  quality and I have to thank my my  colleagues for that so today we're here  to listen to you to hear your views on  the next steps for the European Green  Deal which is the climate law it has to  be your Green Deal it has to be our  Green Deal  so consultation and debate will be and  remain not just today but will remain  essential now let me just remind us of  why this is so important I think that  the scientific evidence is clear tape by  day how quickly and disastrously  consequences of unconstrained climate  change are affecting us we're  increasingly seeing warnings of risks  turning into haunting manifestations of  desire  stur across the globe just look at  Australia wildfires have killed  approximately 1 billion animals 1  billion so sort of double the population  of the European Union it's mind-boggling  and I want to make clear that this is  not me  projecting project fear we're just  reporting on what's actually happening  it is urgent it is essential it is  undeniable that we need to take action  the risks will only increase as the  earth gets hotter and our climate gets  more disturbed climate is changing we  are in a crisis we saw a record number  of cyclones in the Indian Ocean last  year the wettest 12 months on record in  the United States the planet's hottest  August on record and it's happening  close to our home last summer  temperature records were broken in  European countries from Spain to  Slovakia with France recording its  highest ever temperature of forty five  point nine degrees Celsius in southern  Germany the June heatwave hit just after  storms with heavy rain and flooded  streets and cellars and forced the  Munich Airport to suspend its operations  temporarily everyone in Spain is now  very familiar with the phenomenon of  gotough ria which used to happen on an  average of once every 50 years and now  seems to happen almost every year  and even more than once so in November 9  people died as storm swept parts of  France Greece in Italy with flash floods  landslides and the collapse of an  overpass I can go on and on with this  but I think it is good to remind  ourselves of this but without giving in  to paralysis because it's happening and  we can't stop it we can do something  about this this is the most important  thing and then the question is what can  we do as institution as individuals  politicians as businesspeople as  scientists  what can we do people are when I do  interviews very often confronting me  with with a dilemma so what is your  choice do you choose to do something  like climate or do you choose for jobs  do you have any idea what this costs and  I think it is our duty collectively to  explain that choosing to do something  against the climate crisis is choosing  for jobs for future jobs and saying that  choosing to invest in this is much  smarter than not choosing and spending  more money not on investment but on  mitigating or on chale or non at  addressing the challenges which is money  that will be spent but does not bring  any revenues it's not an investment so I  think in our public communication we  will have to concentrate on that to  detect the false contradictions and to  help people understand that doing  nothing does not mean that everything  stays the same that is the biggest trap  in public communication believing that  by doing nothing everything will stay  the same by doing nothing the problem  will get much worse but on the other  side by acting we can actually tackle  the problem because this is in my view  today the biggest biggest challenge it's  increasingly no longer needed to fight  climate deniers  I think climate deniers themselves by  now have understood that they're on the  wrong track and they're not very  convincing but I've seen already the  first climate deniers moving to climate  desperation saying you know why let it  be it's too late let's live it out for a  couple of years let's see what happens  and we saw that we saw some of that last  week also in in Davos  especially one speech of somebody who's  72 years old who was a billionaire and  you know the the issue is that it's its  first generation it's not going to have  severe consequences before the end of  their natural lives if they have a lot  of money they can move to places where  they will be less affected by the  consequences but as a dad of both of two  children from that you can call  Millennials the two children that are  part of Generation Z I listened to those  generations who are thinking about their  future and who are thinking ahead then  you are saying we need to act because  you're playing with our future so I will  not be cornered by people who say  because you you point to the urgency  you're a pessimist and you doom and  gloom we should be optimistic no I think  there is no optimism in denying it  there's no optimism and saying we will  all be okay by doing nothing because  people are too smart to buy that they  might buy just like you know you  sometimes drink too much to forget your  sorrows it is like a way of numbing your  senses by listening to people who deny  all of this but it's like with alcohol  when sooner or later you wake up and you  have a terrible headache and you have to  confront reality you know you you can't  drown your problems and alcohol even  though people will continue to try and  do it and you can't drown these problems  by denying them they will come back even  stronger so in this context what do we  need to do well there's so much we need  to do and and there'll be ample  opportunity to talk about everything we  need to do but my fundamental point is  we can do this we have the science we  have the technology we could certainly  find the money I mean if the Commission  is talking about between let's say 260  and 300 billion  euros yearly to invest in this to make  this transition happen  it sounds daunting but if I was in Davos  last week and I hear what people are  talking about the size of investment  they're thinking about anyway and even  if you just look at the size of  investment that's still being done in  fossil fuels  if you reorient that or at least part of  that into this direction we can find the  money so I repeat the technology the  science the money is not to top so why  is it difficult I think the essential  issue is one of governance is one of  governance how do you organize this like  in any transition especially transition  that is a dual or triple even for Europe  we have the transition because of the  climate crisis Mother Earth is telling  us I can't go on like this and I will  let you feel it if you go on like this  we have an industrial revolution that is  the first in human history that affects  every being on this planet at the same  time never happened before and as  Europeans we also have a demographic  challenge combined with the other two  challenges that changes Europe's  position in the world when my parents  were born Europe accounted for about 23  24 percent of the world's population  Africa 5 in a couple of years time it  will be exactly the opposite that has  huge geopolitical consequences for us  and for everyone and these three  challenges combined will dictate the  policy choices we make and I believe  this is like in any other tectonic  transformation humanity has gone through  is a challenge to everyone and to every  institution 